  "query_preview": "..\nMonday and Tuesday: The threat for fog will continue to increase \nearly Monday morning in the Spokane area and northern mountain \nvalleys as temperatures are near freezing. Fog may become dense with \nvisibility down to 1/4 mile and less. The morning commute may be \nslick with moisture freezing to roadways. Otherwise, expect dry \nconditions under a high pressure ridge. A deep low pressure looms \noff the WA coast, which will send a warm front through the region \nlate in the evening. Snow will increase at Stevens and Washington \nPass around midnight tonight and will spread eastward. There is some \ndisagreement still amongst the models on the eastward extent of \nprecipitation shield, but the best chance for accumulating snow will \nbe in the east slopes of the Cascades. Current thinking is the \nMethow valley, Wenatchee River valley (west of wenatchee), and \nStevens Pass will see around 1-4\" of snow through Tuesday afternoon \nbefore precipitation intensity decreases and temperatures warm to \nnear freezing. From 10 PM Monday to 10 PM Tuesday, Washington Pass \nhas a 50% chance of at least 6 inches of snow and a 15% chance of a \nfoot or more of snow. Have issued a winter weather advisory for \nWestern Okanogan county where confidence is higher for snow causing \ntravel impacts. Those planning to travel for the holidays will want \nto keep a very close eye on the forecast and be prepared for winter \ntravel conditions.\n\nWednesday through Monday: Several areas of deep low pressure off the \nPacific coast will bring increasing snow levels around 4000-5000 \nfeet through Thanksgiving. Washington Pass remains vulnerable to \nsnow impacts, especially this round as PWATs increase to around 200% \nof normal Wednesday night into Thursday. The 10th to 90th percentile \nsnowfall at Washington Pass Wednesday and Thursday is 13 to 17 \ninches, enough to cause travel disruptions on SR-20 for folks \ncrossing the Cascades. Snow levels will favor mostly rain at Stevens \nPass and all rain at Snoqualmie Pass during this period. Sherman \nPass will see around 2 to 5 inches of snow mostly Wednesday night. \nThanksgiving in the valley locations looks fairly wet (especially in \nthe morning) with most locations seeing around 0.10-0.50 inches of \nrain with the greatest amounts in the east slopes of the Cascades, \nnorthern valleys and north Idaho.\n\nFriday and into the weekend trends drier but not completely dry as \nthe INW will be sandwiched between the subtropical jet and polar \njet. A developing surface low northeast of Hawaii and deep low \npressure off the southeast Alaska coast will draw additional \nmoisture towards the INW Friday and into the weekend, delivering\nadditional light rain to much of the region and high mountain \nsnow above 4500-5500 feet to the Cascades and ID Panhandle \nmountains. /DB",
